ex totals, so the size of the funding gap isn't quantified.\n- **Debt, open but costlier:** Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably in Q3 2026. Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp. I found no leverage or FFO/debt figures, so I can't say how much more debt the utilities can take on without ratings pressure.\n- **Equity, weak:** Pellham withdrew its planned C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short. Two other utility share offerings were cut back in Q3. I found no valuation data and no sign that the issuance window is reopening.\n- **Result:** Pellham says it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves. So the shortfall is already reducing spending.\n- **Who is affected:** Norvane looks fundable mainly through debt. Pellham is the one that can't fully fund its plan. It is also the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria, so this is a negative for grid-equipment demand.\n\n**What would change this:** a large Pellham debt deal, credit metrics showing room for more debt, or the equity window reopening. Hybrids, asset sales or partner capital are possible alternatives. They are my suggestions and aren't in the sources.\n\nThis draws on the debt sub-assessment (n5.r1) and the equity sub-assessment (n6.r1).","ttft_ms":1706,"type":"result","duration_ms":31179,"uuid":"231879ec-0030-47fa-8535-564b1532a4c6","ttft_stream_ms":1007,"time_to_request_ms":96,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":7,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":22,"init_frame_send":1,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":16,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":16,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":3,"request_build":4},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_requ